spi: cadence: a master moves a FIFO per interrupt, not one byte

After the first FIFO (128 bytes) of a transfer the interrupt handler moved
THLD bytes for every "TX FIFO under the threshold" interrupt. That is right
for a slave, whose threshold is half the FIFO, but a master keeps the
threshold at 1: every byte past the first 128 took an interrupt of its own.
PX4 reads the ICM-42688 FIFO 204 bytes at a time, 800 times a second: about
65 000 interrupts a second, half of one Cortex-A9 core.

For a master that interrupt means the TX FIFO is empty: every byte written so
far has left it, and all but the one that may still be in the shift register
are in the RX FIFO. Take those and put as many new ones in.

Applies to ADI linux 2e8908932dfd (6.1.70).
---
diff --git a/drivers/spi/spi-cadence.c b/drivers/spi/spi-cadence.c
index aacc9b382..7ed7262e3 100644
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-cadence.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-cadence.c
@@ -402,6 +402,16 @@ static irqreturn_t cdns_spi_irq(int irq, void *dev_id)
 		if (trans_cnt > 1 && xspi->tx_bytes < xspi->tx_fifo_depth >> 1)
 			cdns_spi_write(xspi, CDNS_SPI_THLD, 1);
 
+		/* A master keeps the threshold at one: the TX FIFO is empty
+		 * here, so every byte written so far has left it and all but
+		 * the one that may still be in the shift register are in the
+		 * RX FIFO. Take those and put as many new ones in, instead of
+		 * one byte for each interrupt.
+		 */
+		if (!spi_controller_is_slave(ctlr) &&
+		    xspi->rx_bytes - xspi->tx_bytes > 2)
+			trans_cnt = xspi->rx_bytes - xspi->tx_bytes - 1;
+
 		while (trans_cnt) {
 			cdns_spi_read_rx_fifo(xspi, 1);
 
